      "text": "Okay, you\u0027re looking to use the **Meta Conversions API (CAPI)**. This is the server-side way to send web events, app events, or even offline events directly to Meta\u0027s servers. It complements the Meta Pixel (which runs in the user\u0027s browser) and can help improve data accuracy, especially with browser restrictions like ITP or ad blockers.\n\nHere\u0027s a breakdown and an example:\n\n**Why use the Conversions API?**\n\n1.  **Reliability:** Less affected by browser-based ad blockers, cookie restrictions, or network issues on the client-side.\n2.  **Data Control:** You decide precisely what data to send and when.\n3.  **Full Funnel Visibility:** Send events that happen offline or later in the customer journey (e.g., CRM updates).\n4.  **Improved Measurement \u0026 Attribution:** Can lead to better ad performance by providing Meta with more complete data.\n\n**Key Components \u0026 Parameters:**\n\n1.  **`pixel_id` (Path Parameter):** Your Meta Pixel ID. You\u0027ll send events *to* this Pixel.\n2.  **`access_token` (Query Parameter or Header):** An access token specifically generated for the Conversions API. **Keep this secure!**\n3.  **`data` (Request Body - Array of Event Objects):** This is the main payload. Each object in the array represents a single event.\n    *   **`event_name` (Required):** The name of the event.\n        *   **Standard Events:** `PageView`, `ViewContent`, `AddToCart`, `InitiateCheckout`, `Purchase`, `Lead`, `CompleteRegistration`, `Search`, `Contact`, etc. (Use Meta\u0027s predefined names for these).\n        *   **Custom Events:** Any name you define (e.g., `ClickedUpgradeButton`, `WatchedVideoTutorial`).\n    *   **`event_time` (Required):** Unix timestamp (seconds since Jan 1, 1970, UTC) of when the event occurred.\n    *   **`user_data` (Highly Recommended for Matching):** Information about the user who performed the event. **All PII (Personally Identifiable Information) MUST be hashed (SHA256) before sending