      "text": "Okay, you want to send Meta Conversions API (CAPI) events using JavaScript.\n\n**VERY IMPORTANT SECURITY WARNING:**\n\nYou **SHOULD NOT** make direct calls to the Meta Conversions API from client-side JavaScript (i.e., JavaScript running in a user\u0027s web browser).\nDoing so would require embedding your `ACCESS_TOKEN` directly in your client-side code. This token is highly sensitive. If exposed, anyone could send events to your Pixel, potentially polluting your data or exhausting your API limits.\n\n**The Correct Approach (Server-Side JavaScript - Node.js):**\n\nThe standard and secure way to use the Conversions API is from your server. Your client-side JavaScript can collect event information and send it to your own backend server, and then your backend server (e.g., using Node.js) securely makes the call to Meta\u0027s API.\n\nHere\u0027s an example using Node.js:\n\n**1. Setup your Node.js project:**\n\n```bash\nmkdir meta-capi-node-example\ncd meta-capi-node-example\nnpm init -y\nnpm install node-fetch crypto  # node-fetch for making HTTP requests (or use built-in https/http or axios)\n                               # crypto for hashing\n```\n\n**2. Create your JavaScript file (e.g., `sendEvent.js`):**\n\n```javascript\n// sendEvent.js\nconst fetch \u003d require(\u0027node-fetch\u0027); // Or use axios: const axios \u003d require(\u0027axios\u0027);\nconst crypto \u003d require(\u0027crypto\u0027);\n\n// --- CONFIGURATION (Store these securely, e.g., environment variables) ---\nconst PIXEL_ID \u003d \"YOUR_PIXEL_ID\"; // Replace with your Pixel ID\nconst ACCESS_TOKEN \u003d \"YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN\"; // Replace with your CAPI Access Token\n// For testing, get this from Events Manager \u003e Your Pixel \u003e Test Events\n// const TEST_EVENT_CODE \u003d \"TESTXXXXX\"; // Uncomment and use for testing\n\n// --- USER \u0026 EVENT DATA (This would typically come from your application logic) ---\nconst userEmail \u003d \"user@example.com\";\nconst userPhoneNumber \u003d \"+11234567890\"; // E.164 format recommended\nconst clientIpAddress \u003d \"123.123.123.123\"; // User\u0027s actual IP (obtained from request headers on your server)\nconst clientUserAgent \u003d \"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) ...\"; // User\u0027s actual User Agent\nconst eventSourceUrl \u003d \"https://www.yoursite.com/product-page\";\nconst eventTimeUnix \u003d Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000); // Current time as Unix timestamp\n\n// --- HELPER FOR HASHING (SHA256) ---\nfunction sha256Hash(text) {\n    return crypto.createHash(\u0027sha256\u0027).update(text).digest(\u0027hex\u0027);\n}\n\nasync function sendMetaEvent() {\n    const eventData \u003d {\n        event_name: \"ViewContent\", // Example: User viewed a product page\n        event_time: eventTimeUnix,\n        action_source: \"website\",\n        event_source_url: eventSourceUrl,\n        event_id: `viewcontent_${Date.now()}`, // Example unique event ID\n        user_data: {\n            em: sha256Hash(userEmail.toLowerCase()), // Hash email\n            ph: sha256Hash(userPhoneNumber),       // Hash phone\n            // fn: sha256Hash(\"John\".toLowerCase()),  // Example: Hash first name\n            // ln: sha256Hash(\"Doe\".toLowerCase()),   // Example: Hash last name\n            client_ip_address: clientIpAddress,\n            client_user_agent: clientUserAgent,\n            // fbp: \"fb.1.1598212827974.2345678901\", // If you have it (e.g., from a cookie)\n            // fbc: \"fb.1.1554763741205.AbCdEfGhIjKlMnOpQrStUvWxYz\", // If you have it (e.g., from URL param)\n        },\n        custom_data: {\n            content_name: \"Awesome T-Shirt\",\n            content_category: \"Apparel\",\n            content_ids: [\"SKU123\"],\n            content_type: \"product\",\n            value: 19.99, // Optional: value of the content viewed\n            currency: \"USD\" // Optional: currency\n        }\n    };\n\n    const payload \u003d {\n        data: [eventData], // API expects an array of event objects\n        // test_event_code: TEST_EVENT_CODE // Uncomment for testing\n    };\n\n    const url \u003d `https://graph.facebook.com/v19.0/${PIXEL_ID}/events?access_token\u003d${ACCESS_TOKEN}`;\n    // For API v19.0. Adjust version as needed.\n\n    console.log(\"Sending payload:\", JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2));\n\n    try {\n        const response \u003d await fetch(url, {\n            method: \u0027POST\u0027,\n            headers: {\n                \u0027Content-Type\u0027: \u0027application/json\u0027\n            },\n            body: JSON.stringify(payload)\n        });\n\n        const responseData \u003d await response.json();\n\n        if (!response.ok) {\n            console.error(\"Error sending event:\", responseData);\n            throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}, message: ${JSON.stringify(responseData)}`);\n        }\n\n        console.log(\"Event sent successfully!\");\n        console.log(\"Response:\", responseData);\n        return responseData;\n\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error(\"Failed to send event to Meta API:\", error);\n        if (error.response) {\n            console.error(\"Error details:\", await error.response.text());\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Call the function to send the event\nsendMetaEvent();\n\n// Example: If this were an Express.js route handler\n// app.post(\u0027/track-event\u0027, async (req, res) \u003d\u003e {\n//     // 1. Get event details from req.body\n//     // 2. Get user IP from req.ip or req.headers[\u0027x-forwarded-for\u0027]\n//     // 3. Get user agent from req.headers[\u0027user-agent\u0027]\n//     // 4. Construct eventData similar to above\n//     // 5. Call sendMetaEvent (or inline the logic)\n//     // 6. Send response to client\n// });\n```\n\n**To run this Node.js example:**\n\n1.  Save the code as `sendEvent.js`.\n2.  Replace `YOUR_PIXEL_ID` and `YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN` with your actual credentials.\n3.  (Optional for testing) Get a `TEST_EVENT_CODE` from your Pixel\u0027s \"Test Events\" tab in Meta Events Manager and uncomment the line.\n4.  Run from your terminal: `node sendEvent.js`\n\n**Client-Side (Browser) Data Collection to Your Server:**\n\nYour browser JavaScript would collect the necessary data and send it to an endpoint on your Node.js server (or any other backend).\n\n```html\n\u003c!-- Example in an HTML file --\u003e\n\u003cbutton id\u003d\"trackViewContentButton\"\u003eTrack View Content\u003c/button\u003e\n\n\u003cscript\u003e\ndocument.getElementById(\u0027trackViewContentButton\u0027).addEventListener(\u0027click\u0027, async () \u003d\u003e {\n    const eventDetails \u003d {\n        eventName: \"ViewContent\",\n        eventSourceUrl: window.location.href,\n        // Add other relevant data you can collect client-side\n        // like product IDs, names, values, etc.\n        product: {\n            name: \"Awesome T-Shirt\",\n            id: \"SKU123\",\n            price: 19.99,\n            currency: \"USD\"\n        }\n        // DO NOT send sensitive user PII (like raw email) directly\n        // unless your server endpoint is specifically designed to handle and hash it.\n    };\n\n    try {\n        // Send data to YOUR backend endpoint\n        const response \u003d await fetch(\u0027/api/track-meta-event\u0027, { // Your server\u0027s endpoint\n            method: \u0027POST\u0027,\n            headers: {\n                \u0027Content-Type\u0027: \u0027application/json\u0027\n            },\n            body: JSON.stringify(eventDetails)\n        });\n\n        if (response.ok) {\n            console.log(\u0027Event data sent to server successfully.\u0027);\n            const result \u003d await response.json();\n            console.log(\u0027Server response:\u0027, result);\n        } else {\n            console.error(\u0027Failed to send event data to server.\u0027);\n        }\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error(\u0027Error sending event data:\u0027, error);\n    }\n});\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\n\nYour backend endpoint (e.g., `/api/track-meta-event` in Express.js) would then receive this data, add server-side information (like user IP, user agent), hash any PII, and then make the secure call to the Meta CAPI as shown in the Node.js example above.\n\n**Necessary Parameters (Recap):**\n\n*   **`PIXEL_ID`** (in URL path): Your Meta Pixel ID.\n*   **`access_token`** (in URL query or Authorization header): Your Conversions API access token.\n*   **`data`** (JSON request body - an array of event objects):\n    *   **`event_name` (Required):** e.g., `ViewContent`, `AddToCart`, `Purchase`, `Lead`.\n    *   **`event_time` (Required):** Unix timestamp (seconds).\n    *   **`action_source` (Required):** e.g., `website`, `app`, `physical_store`.\n    *   **`user_data` (Highly Recommended for matching):**\n        *   Hashed PII: `em` (email), `ph` (phone), `fn` (first name), `ln` (last name), etc.\n        *   `client_ip_address`: User\u0027s IP.\n        *   `client_user_agent`: User\u0027s browser/device agent.\n        *   `fbp`, `fbc`: Meta browser and click ID cookies (if available).\n        *   `external_id`: Your internal user ID.\n    *   **`event_source_url` (Highly Recommended):** URL where the event occurred.\n    *   **`custom_data` (Optional):** Event-specific parameters like `value`, `currency`, `content_ids`, `order_id`.\n    *   **`event_id` (Optional, but Recommended for Deduplication):** A unique ID for this specific event instance.\n\nBy using a server-side approach, you keep your `ACCESS_TOKEN` secure and have more control over the data being sent.",
