ts Manager (Test Events tab if `TEST_EVENT_CODE` is set).\n            \u003c/p\u003e\n          \u003c/header\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      );\n    }\n\n    export default App;\n    ```\n\n4.  **Run your React app:**\n    ```bash\n    npm start\n    # or if using Vite:\n    # npm run dev\n    ```\n    It will usually start on `http://localhost:3000`.\n\n**How it works:**\n\n1.  User interacts with your React app (e.g., views a page, clicks a button).\n2.  React event handler calls `trackMetaEvent` with relevant data.\n3.  `trackMetaEvent` makes a `fetch` POST request to your backend API endpoint (`/api/track-meta-event` on `localhost:3001`).\n4.  Your Express backend receives the request.\n5.  The backend:\n    *   Adds server-side information (IP, User-Agent, current time).\n    *   Hashes any PII (like email) received from the client.\n    *   Constructs the final payload for the Meta Conversions API.\n    *   Sends the payload to `https://graph.facebook.com/...` using its secure `ACCESS_TOKEN`.\n6.  The backend sends a response back to your React app.\n\nThis setup ensures your `ACCESS_TOKEN` remains secure on your server, and you have full control over the data sent to Meta. Remember to use the \"Test Events\" feature in your Meta Pixel settings to verify events are being received correctly, especially when `TEST_EVENT_CODE` is active.",
